  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests whether adding learned syntactic templates to harmful prompts can bypass refusals, using refusal rates rather than the original studies' fine-tuning or per-token KL measurements.",
    "gate_note": "The relevant quantitative result is Table 3: refusal rates for 1000 WildJailbreak requests on OLMo-2-7B-Instruct after adding chain-of-thought or math templates as prefixes or suffixes. The paper also gives qualitative security examples. Its synthetic fine-tuning and other syntax-domain evaluations do not test safety fine-tuning, response-position KL, safety-localized weights, or deepening safety training.",
    "test_reason": "The refusal experiment (Table 3) adds syntactic templates borrowed from chain-of-thought or math training data as prompt prefixes or suffixes. The authors frame this as exploiting spurious syntax-domain correlations. It is not prefilling the response opening, an adversarial suffix meant to force a compliant start, or a decoding change. So it does not bear on V4's claim that safety fails once the opening is passed. No fine-tuning-on-harm, per-token KL, localization or deepening results appear. Qi et al. (2406.05946) is only cited. This is borderline, because V4 lists 'suffixes'; if editors read V4 as any prompt suffix, the rows give mixed results. The finding is recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Partly wrong. The authors conclude that learned syntax-domain spurious correlations can bypass refusals, a security risk beyond 'just another jailbreak'. Chain-of-thought templates cut refusal in most settings, including exact prefix from 40% to 2.5%. They say math templates reduce refusal 'to a less severe degree', dropping only in synonym and antonym prefix settings. Table 3 shows math templates raised refusal in the other 8 settings, which the record's 'weaker and less consistent' understates.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses syntactic-template prefixes and suffixes as the intervention and refusal rate as the measure; it does not use harmful-example fine-tuning, compliant-opening prefills, or per-token KL measurement.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The effect varies with template domain (chain-of-thought versus math), prefix versus suffix placement, and prompt perturbation setting.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On OLMo-2-7B-Instruct (1000 WildJailbreak requests, baseline refusal 0.40), chain-of-thought template prefixes and suffixes from training data lowered refusal in 9 of 10 settings (exact prefix 0.025). Math templates raised refusal in 8 of 10 settings (up to 0.785). The paper also gives qualitative bypasses on GPT-4o-mini.",
        "proposed_claim": "Syntactic templates from refusal-free training domains, added to harmful prompts, reduce refusal via learned syntax-domain correlations; the effect depends on template domain.",
