    "peer_reviewed": "Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)",
    "problems": "The quantitative refusal-rate experiment is reported for one model. No uncertainty estimates are given. The reported results do not establish that every template or suffix bypasses refusal.",
    "rows_left_out": "No rows omitted.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "Compliant-opening prefills, adversarial (optimized) suffixes, decoding changes, and any per-position analysis of where refusal fails."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "Single model; no uncertainty reported. Baseline refusal is only 40% on WildJailbreak requests, so 60% compliance exists without any template. Harmful-output examples (App. F) are qualitative. The reader's labels misgroup r5, a refusal decrease, with increases. If editors decide prompt-side suffixes fall under V4, the results are mixed: chain-of-thought templates mostly reduce refusal, while math templates mostly increase it."
  },
